— the disposition of that git-failure arm is only implicitly 'degrade to walk' and the plan does not distinguish 'not a git work tree' from 'git present but ls-files errored', so a transient/partial git failure silently re-enables scanning ignored files (the exact defect being fixed) without surfacing it.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f0586ecd6f5e2f2be","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach: 'Inside a git work tree (root / \".git\" exists and git ls-files exits 0) ... otherwise, including when git is missing, it falls back to the current rglob walk.'","norm_id":"n7a3497f905a29334","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A git work tree where `git ls-files` exits non-zero transiently or partially: helper silently falls back to the walk and resumes judging .rebar/scratch ignored files — the bug recurs with no signal."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Specify that `git ls-files` is only trusted on exit 0; on a non-zero exit when `.git` IS present, raise/surface (fatal) rather than silently falling back, so a git error cannot re-enable ignored-file scanning unnoticed.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.375,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Either factor the shared git-ls-files/fallback-walk primitive so both `check_criteria_vocabulary.py` and `_repo_files.files_named` call it, or state in the plan why a parallel implementation (different contract) is the deliberate choice over reuse.","cohort":["A1"],"criteria":["A1"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: \"Follow the existing precedent in scripts/check_criteria_vocabulary.py: _git_tracked_relatives (line 171) ... iter_repository_files (line 208) falls back to _walk_repository_files (line 218)\"","Plan: \"check_criteria_vocabulary.py is left as is.\"","check_criteria_vocabulary.py lines 171-215 contain the identical git-detect / ls-files -z / os.walk-fallback structure the new helper will reproduce"],"finding":"The git-work-tree detection, `git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard -z` call, and rglob/os.walk fallback that `files_named` will contain already exist in `scripts/check_criteria_vocabulary.py` (`_git_tracked_relatives` line 171, `iter_repository_files` line 208, `_walk_repository_files` line 218). The plan re-implements this mechanism in a second module rather than factoring the shared git-listing primitive. The two helpers do have different public contracts (tuple-yielding + built-in exclusion/symlink filters vs. name-pattern + per-caller excluded_dirs), so a new helper is defensible, but the plan should either extract the shared git-listing core both can call or state affirmatively why a parallel copy is preferable, so the duplication is a deliberate, documented choice rather than drift.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"ffaccf3df122d449d","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach section: `scripts/_repo_files.py` (new) `files_named`","norm_id":"n3ed4118b3027286b","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false